  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1120 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nModels continue to show some lingering showers through Sunday\nmorning in the wake of the cold front tracking through the area\nover the weekend. Precipitation chances remain low at this time\n(20-30 percent) CAMs are focusing on low POPs (20-30%) across\nportions of the Northwest Hill County, Heartland, and Northern\nEdwards Plateau. Expect cooler and drier conditions to settle into\nWest Central Texas behind the cold front on Sunday, and a\nreinforcing shortwave on Tuesday keeping the cooler conditions\naround through the end of next week. High temperatures in the mid\n50s to mid 60s are expected Sunday, which looks to be the warmest\nday in the long term forecast. Temperatures will gradually become\ncooler behind the reinforcing surge of cold air mentioned above,\nkeeping high temperatures in the 50s and lows in the 30s for much\nof next week next week. \n\nModels are still struggling with regards to any precipitation \ndeveloping late next week and into next weekend. The GFS shows a \nlarge closed off upper low near El Paso and a large area of \nrainfall across West and Central Texas, with the ECMWF much weaker\nand progressive and a largely dry forecast. At this point, will \nlet the model blends split the difference and show some small POPs\nacross the area.",
